From b8264afc42d11f1213fdf608d3b0dd4011e6fe21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matthew Dowell <mbd06b+github@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:23:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] fix: rev-parse keeps Brit's conventions; usage errors
 survive a closed stderr

Re-review small items 4 to 7.

- rev-parse: `--path-format relative` as two arguments is accepted again
  after the first revision, and `--help` after a revision shows Brit's
  help again unless it follows `--` (the spelling's parser reports
  DisplayHelp; brit-cli's `subcommand_help()` prints the subcommand's
  long help with clap's status).
- Diagnostics on error paths no longer panic or turn into a closed-reader
  write: `check-ref-format` usage, `-C` failure, `for-each-ref`'s broken-ref
  warning use non-panicking writes; `merge-base --is-ancestor`, `rev-list`
  and rev-parse's object-not-found note ignore a failed stderr write and
  keep their own status. With a closed stderr, `check-ref-format` and
  `-C missing` exited 101 (panic) and `merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD` /
  `rev-list --count` exited 141; now 129, 128, 129, 129.
- Docs: the broken_pipe journey says this change never gives a mutating
  command 141 and that push's own write order is outside it; the `-C`
  help says recognised subcommands only.
- Known differences recorded: `-1` and `--all` refused, a lone `--`
  dropped, an ambiguous prefix losing the resolver diagnostic (rev-parse);
  `merge-base` with no argument exits 2; `rev-list --count <rev>^{tree}`
  exits 128 where Git prints 0; lone `ls-remote -h` (already recorded).

Journeys: `rev_parse_keeps_brits_conventions_after_a_revision` and the
closed-stderr usage cases in `a_closed_reader_never_replaces_a_genuine_failure`,
both failing on e534083f0's binary.

